
day 14 summary 




Orchestrators — coordinate which agent does what and in what order
Memory layers — short-term (context window), long-term (vector stores, databases)
Tool infrastructure — APIs, sandboxes, MCP servers that agents can call
Multiple agents — specialized sub-agents handed tasks by an orchestrator
Guardrails and evals — safety checks, output validation, retry logic
Human-in-the-loop hooks — approval steps before irreversible actions

A useful analogy: an agent is like a single employee. An agentic system is like the company — the org chart, communication channels, policies, tools, and workflows that make a team of employees actually productive.
In practice, the line can blur. A single powerful agent with many tools can resemble a simple agentic system. But when people say "agentic system" they typically mean a designed pipeline or network — often multi-agent — where the architecture itself is doing meaningful work beyond what any one agent could do alone.
The distinction matters most when you're building: one agent is simpler to debug and control; an agentic system unlocks parallelism and specialization but introduces coordination complexity and more failure points.

